;">THIRSTY WORK</h2></div></td></tr>
<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Considering he is a lifelong Everton fan, =
it=E2=80=99s no surprise that Andy Burnham seems to be in favour of allowin=
g <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFu=
ifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPgV-S1dBhJIId5JcVIQI73f3=
np7UuAl3tMGBJm3B1CO5FXk-Gmlq8XgHvK6eDeEpSTL76_rTpzD-pHEHUb570UpSUH_O_H-Vc62=
BOcqAKtekjHyvIZ52ePmlBcvGMgztE774n5LI0jIYjPgtwMasLUhPs1knOsvpp7SNjm0uLVxzFl=
foenzfEf4F6sMjFsnRo2MMalJXwDM94Ac2iox11_6X_lpYHrtsjBG9Hze1eJWGl1mXjYqdXbcjv=
5ok9kXrdn/4ub/5Z1uOrHCRgKnlB96ciyXmg/h28/h001.wYZtJ2MK7i6s-tMdQ-PYZoUiiUCRy=
cJ73SsLnmyhhVA" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">fans to drink Tin at football mat=
ches</a>. Bouts of boardroom turmoil, points deductions and assorted relega=
tion battles mean the lot of an Evertonian is not always a happy one. The P=
rime Minister believes that if he and other fans fancy a sip or two to help=
 ease the pain of watching Thierno Barry head wide of an open goal, then th=
at ought to be their constitutional right as proud Englishmen. Sadly, no su=
ch right currently exists, as alcohol consumption within view of the pitch =
has been outlawed in the top five divisions of English football <a href=3D"=
https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTN=
tZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPgoktwUMJBcIgbvI7_YFnZaCexxN6yVj6mC6X=
tzrHZiA6lVgs9tb2FAbz4NV5zeWNLmbWvmEgqJlxt9dFGeAhhKITTIVj2_81kuvfN9PgbXcNOk7=
-m2MxF9-x5FbYUHU1A2IagBZxUD1LMG6SwMt4a0Z0nIzrP0TJ8seojMXBPr-Yk8qR8e-ykG0gvk=
K20Ty6MZhwB1qBTwoUzk8_g4k-UANrZpmzyIX9xM4JIFBF0URXx4WkL4TFVr_RKeh4yMDRv/4ub=
/5Z1uOrHCRgKnlB96ciyXmg/h29/h001.hZOorQD6UphzBegX8v_XmJZeaGwLhWzK1EAJrcQou7=
0" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">since the troubled times of the 1980s</a>. Ins=
tead, thirsty match-goers are forced to endure an agonising wait of 45 whol=
e minutes before rushing to the bar, joining long queues of fellow fans eag=
er to buy pints of foaming shaft they must subsequently chug back, yard-of-=
ale style, in enough time to return to their seats for the start of the sec=
ond half.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In a move some cynical folk have described=
 as populist political grandstanding, Burnham has correctly pointed out tha=
t in not being allowed to enjoy a pint during games, football fans are bein=
g discriminated against compared to their counterparts in sports such as cr=
icket, rugby and horse racing. =E2=80=9CI don=E2=80=99t think it=E2=80=99s =
fair for football fans to be singled out in that way and I made that point,=
=E2=80=9D he told a gaggle of reporters during a flight to New York. =E2=80=
=9CThat legislation dates to an era where football was very different and I=
 do believe there is a case to remove that discrimination. There are differ=
ent ways it could be done =E2=80=93 it could happen on a trial basis. But I=
=E2=80=99m not going to back away from what=E2=80=99s been a long-held view=
 of mine. I don=E2=80=99t see how it can be fair that some supporters of on=
e sport can be treated differently.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">While Burnham has a point, reaction to the=
 possibility of booze returning to football match stands has been mixed. Wh=
ile the sanctimonious bleating of =E2=80=9Cif you can=E2=80=99t last 45 min=
utes without a drink, then there=E2=80=99s something wrong with you=E2=80=
=9D from the puritan brigade strikes Football Daily as reason enough for an=
 immediate change in legislation and trebles all round, reintroducing alcoh=
ol could pose a logistical problem not previously experienced by the curren=
t generation of fans. Specifically, them being forced to repeatedly stand u=
p and sit down again in order to make room for an endless procession of peo=
ple going to and from the bars and toilets. While we=E2=80=99re not proud t=
o admit it, Football Daily has attended enough rugby internationals to know=
 exactly how annoying this can be.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The Football Supporters Association favour=
s Burnham=E2=80=99s suggestion that allowing fans to bring an overpriced, f=
limsy receptacle of flat lager to their seats would massively enhance the m=
atchday experience and the Premier League is obviously open to any idea tha=
t helps its members prise an extra few quid out of customers. The UK Footba=
ll Policing Unit, however, is understandably less enthused. =E2=80=9COther =
sports simply don=E2=80=99t see the violence and criminal behaviour from th=
eir supporters that football does, albeit a minority, with alcohol often pl=
aying a significant part in violent and disorderly behaviour,=E2=80=9D they=
 harrumphed. =E2=80=9CIt would be irresponsible to fuel this by allowing gr=
eater alcohol consumption during games.=E2=80=9D While alcohol consumption =
increases the risk of violent disorder, the recent box-park phenomenon of p=
erformative and celebratory pint-throwing almost certainly will too. Nobody=
 wants to come home from a football match drenched in beer, even if the onl=
y alternatives are to stay at home or start supporting Spurs.</p>
